.TH CYAML_NODE_TAG 3 "2026-08-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_node_tag \- return a node's YAML tag
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

const char *cyaml_node_tag(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the YAML tag of node, or NULL when node carries no tag.

When the parser reads a node that has an explicit tag in the
document, this gives the fully resolved tag string, whether the
document wrote it as "!!str", a custom "!foo", a shorthand that %TAG
resolves, or a verbatim tag.  The tag is one of the seven
CYAML_TAG_* constants (CYAML_TAG_NULL, _BOOL, _INT, _FLOAT, _STR,
_SEQ, _MAP) or a custom tag string.  A node with no tag gives NULL,
and so does a node whose type comes only from implicit core-schema
resolution.

A dictionary key can never carry a tag, because the keys of this DOM
are plain strings and not nodes (see cyaml_dictionary_get(3)).  A tag
on a key in the document types the key exactly as it would type a
value, and the dictionary stores the canonical text of the typed
key, but there is nowhere to keep the tag itself.

The node owns the tag string and the caller must not free it.  The
pointer is borrowed and stays valid until the node's tag or value is
set again or the node is destroyed, so any of these ends it: a
cyaml_node_set_tag(3) on the node, a cyaml_set(3) that reaches the
node (a new value drops a core tag that doesn't match it), a
cyaml_list_remove(3), cyaml_dictionary_remove(3) or cyaml_delete(3)
of the node, a cyaml_dictionary_set(3) that replaces it, and a
destroy of its tree.  Copy the text if you need it longer.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  A DOM node handle.  It may be NULL, and the function then
      returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A borrowed tag string that the caller doesn't own, or NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_node_set_tag (3),
.BR cyaml_type (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_get (3)
